> How can I send an email from python? I just want to send it to myself, so it doesn't need to be particular professional looking, as long as it doesn't get filtered out as spam.
**assistant**:
> Sending an email from Python can be done using the built-in `smtplib` library, along with `email` for crafting the message. Here's a simple example using a secure connection with Gmail's SMTP server, but you can adjust it for other providers by changing the SMTP server and port.
> 
> First, make sure you have access to your email account through the SMTP protocol—this often involves setting up an "app password" if you have two-factor authentication enabled.
> 
> ```python
> import smtplib
> from email.mime.text import MIMEText
> from email.mime.multipart import MIMEMultipart
> 
> # Define your email credentials and addresses
> sender_email = "your_email@gmail.com"
> receiver_email = "your_email@gmail.com"  # Sending to yourself
> password = "your_app_password"  # Use an app password for Gmail
> 
> # Create the email content
> subject = "Test Email from Python"
> body = "Hello, this is a test email sent from a Python script."
> 
> # Create a MIMEText email object
> message = MIMEMultipart()
> message["From"] = sender_email
> message["To"] = receiver_email
> message["Subject"] = subject
> message.attach(MIMEText(body, "plain"))
> 
> # Connect to the Gmail SMTP server and send the email
> try:
>     with smtplib.SMTP_SSL("smtp.gmail.com", 465) as server:
>         server.login(sender_email, password)
>         server.sendmail(sender_email, receiver_email, message.as_string())
>     print("Email sent successfully!")
> except Exception as e:
>     print(f"Error: {e}")
> ```
> 
> ### Tips to Avoid Spam Filters:
> 1. **Simple Content**: Ensure that your email body doesn't contain typical spam content.
> 2. **Proper Headers**: Make sure the "From", "To", and "Subject" fields are properly filled.
> 3. **Consistent Domains**: If possible, make sure the sending domain matches the sending server's domain.
> 
> ### Important Notes:
> - **App Passwords**: If using Gmail, you'll need to generate an app password under your Google Account security settings.
> - **Security**: Avoid hardcoding your email password in scripts, especially if sharing code. Consider using environment variables or secure vaults for sensitive information.
> 
> This script sends a basic plain-text email. If you want to send more complex emails, such as those with attachments or HTML content, you can adjust the MIME types and incorporate those features as needed.
