**assistant**:
> You can easily create a simple notification on a Mac using the `osascript` command from within a Python script. This allows you to show a macOS notification without needing to manage emails. Here's an example that sends a notification:
> 
> ### Using Python to Send macOS Notifications
> 
> ```python
> import subprocess
> 
> def send_notification(title, message):
>     script = f'display notification "{message}" with title "{title}"'
>     subprocess.run(["osascript", "-e", script])
> 
> # Example usage
> send_notification("Test Notification", "Hello, this is a test notification from Python!")
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation:
> - **`subprocess.run`**: This function executes the command provided. Here, we're running `osascript`, which is a command-line utility for executing AppleScript.
> - **`display notification`**: This command in AppleScript triggers the notification center on macOS.
> 
> ### Running the Script
> Simply run the Python script above using Python 3 on your Mac, and you should see a notification pop up with your specified title and message.
> 
> ### Customization:
> You can easily customize the title and message to whatever you prefer, and this is a lightweight and effective way to receive notifications on your Mac without involving emails.
